================================================================================
Collective variables used in the metadynamics simulation of the
ACE-CYS-NME capped dipeptide in vacuum at T = 300.0 K with the AMBER
ff14SB force field (no non-bonded cutoff, OpenMM Reference platform).
================================================================================

The 23-atom system is built from data/dipeptide.pdb.  Residue labelling
follows the PDB order: ACE = residue 1 (atoms HH31, CH3, HH32, HH33, C, O),
CYS = residue 2 (N, H, CA, HA, CB, HB2, HB3, SG, HG, C, O),
NME = residue 3 (N, H, CH3, HH31, HH32, HH33).

CV 1: phi  (phi backbone dihedral of residue CYS)
        atom indices (0-based)  : (4, 6, 8, 15)
        atom names              : (C of ACE, N of CYS, CA of CYS, C of CYS)
        PDB serials (1-based)   : (5, 7, 9, 16)
        periodicity             : 2*pi (360 degrees), range covered in FES: [-180, +180)
        definition              : IUPAC dihedral formula
                                    phi = atan2( n1 x n2 ),
                                   n1 = (b2) x (b1 - b2),
                                   n2 = (b3 - b2) x (b3),
                                   b1 = N - C_ACE,  b2 = CA - N,  b3 = C - CA.

CV 2: psi  (psi backbone dihedral of residue CYS)
        atom indices (0-based)  : (6, 8, 15, 17)
        atom names              : (N of CYS, CA of CYS, C of CYS, N of NME)
        PDB serials (1-based)   : (7, 9, 16, 18)
        periodicity             : 2*pi (360 degrees), range covered in FES: [-180, +180)
        definition              : IUPAC dihedral formula
                                    psi = atan2( m1 x m2 ),
                                   m1 = (b2) x (b1 - b2),
                                   m2 = (b3 - b2) x (b3),
                                   b1 = CA - N,  b2 = C - CA, b3 = N_NME - C.

Why phi and psi (and not omega)?
    The Ramachandran coordinates (phi, psi) of residue CYS are the
    biologically meaningful backbone degrees of freedom that span all
    canonical secondary-structure basins.  In the ACE-CYS-NME dipeptide
    omega (the peptide bond dihedral, here C_ACE-N-CA-C of residue CYS at
    indices (4, 6, 8, 15)) is constrained rigidly to the trans value
    +-180 degrees by the AMBER ff14SB planarity/periodicity term
    (k_omega ~ 10 kcal/mol with n=2 multiplet) and the lack of
    donor/acceptor to break this barrier in vacuum.  Sampling omega
    would not change because the energy gap between cis and trans is
    about 10 kcal/mol and Metadynamics with hills of width 0.1 rad
    on omega would never overcome it in any feasible run length.
    Using omega as a CV would ruthlessly waste degrees of freedom
    and miss the actual conformational free-energy landscape that
    the Ramachandran plot (phi, psi) is designed to display.

Why a Hill sigma = 0.10 rad (about 5.73 degrees)?
    The smallest barrier between known basins (C7eq <-> alpha_R) is
    about 5-7 kJ/mol at the phi=psi=0 ridge.  A sigma significantly
    smaller under-smooths the FES and creates fictitious wells.  A
    sigma significantly larger (~>=15 degrees) makes individual hills
    indistinguishable from the natural basin widths and slows
    convergence considerably.  0.10 rad is the standard choice for
    alanine/CYS dipeptides in vacuum (Barducci, Bonomi, Parrinello,
    WIREs Comput. Mol. Sci. 1, 826 (2011)).

Hill height w0, bias factor gamma, deposition stride:
    Hill height w0     = 1.0 kJ/mol (initial height for WT-MetaD).
    Bias factor   gamma = 10 (effective sampling temperature
                                gamma*T/(gamma-1) ~ 333 K, a standard
                                alanine-dipeptide compromise).
    Deposition stride  : every 500 dynamics steps  dt = 2 fs
                          => 1 hill/ps.

Boundary conditions:
    No bias cutoff or wall is applied to (phi, psi); the periodic
    range [-pi, pi) is tiled by 72 cells of width 5 degrees each,
    cell-centred grid points at -pi + (i+0.5)*5 degrees
    (i = 0..71).  The wrap-around is handled analytically inside the
    Gaussian evaluation; no minimum-image artefacts can appear.
